A||||25||ramzy_steele||88 formula vs sister's 84 high performance 4||||1||
Z||000000||ramzy_steele||03-20-2001||12:47 PM||||Im about to buy another car and i love fieros as i had the one my sister had before i sold it.  I am about to buy a 88 formula now but am dreading the horrible possiblility of being able to get whooped by my sister.  She jsut has  hgih performance 4 put in hers putting out like 180 horses.  this is more then the 6 i believe but the 88 has a 5 spd compared to her 4.  Could her car actually beat me?  I imagine the 6 may have more torque btu im nto sure and the 5th gear will help.  I CANT ALOW THIS TO HAPPEN OR MY SISTER WILL LAUGH AT ME FOR YEARS!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-20-2001||12:53 PM||||Okay, calm down....<P><BR>What exactly does she have on her four cylinder? Unless she has gone to very far and very expensive extremes she probably would be able to keep up with Formula. If she can beat a Formula than she has a mighty Indy indeed!||||reg||4
Z||000002||ramzy_steele||03-20-2001||12:55 PM||||All I knwo she bought a rebuilt crate enigne that puts out around 180 horses 2.5.  Has stock injectors and everytihng else i believe||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||03-20-2001||01:46 PM||||If it's really putting out 180HP she will most likely kick your butt in a stock Formula in 0-60 and 1/4 mile. The 4 speed is probably actually going to make her even faster on take off. Most 84 4 speeds had a 4.10 final drive ratio.<P>You should probably avoid a drag race...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ramzy_steele||03-20-2001||02:09 PM||||crap this is horrible.  Okay so lets say i have the formula if i added like anotehr 20 horses or so could i take her?  and for that is that like only a performance chip?<P>Will the torque of the v-8 not help me enough although she will have like 20 extra horses or so?||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceCooker||03-20-2001||02:17 PM||||She is a woman<BR>You are a man<P><BR>You will win on driver skill  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||stimpy||03-20-2001||02:17 PM||||Anybody else a little skeptical about a normally aspirated 180 hp duke? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||03-20-2001||02:34 PM||||The 2.5L 4's do not have strong enough lower ends to reliably maintain 180hp. Of course if she bought a "crate" motor are you sure its a 2.5L? The 2.4L quad4's with a 16-valve head put out 160-180. Or if the 2.5 has an "SD" on it. (Kansas Racing Products is now making and selling SD4 engines).<BR>She also could have a 2.5L block from the late 70's (4-bolt mains) and had it custom built for Fiero use. My bet is it's a Quad4 if it's that high in hp.<BR>A 180hp Quad4 hooked to an 84 4:10 ratio 4-speed could beat out a V-6 5-spd. Remember shifting abilities of both drivers is a factor too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||03-20-2001||04:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ramzy_steele:<BR><B>crap this is horrible.  Okay so lets say i have the formula if i added like anotehr 20 horses or so could i take her?  and for that is that like only a performance chip?<P>Will the torque of the v-8 not help me enough although she will have like 20 extra horses or so?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You will NOT see 20 extra horsepower from a chip.<BR>If you want to keep up with 180 hp, you will need to do at least a 3.4 swap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DRH||03-20-2001||05:35 PM||||Good point stimpy... I was a little suspicious too.<P>ramzy, I'm sure a lot of people on here would be interested in hearing more about that 180HP 2.5... That's almost DOUBLE the stock HP... Kinda hard to do with just swapping a crate engine with stock intake/exhaust...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||MrPBody||03-20-2001||08:34 PM||||Yes, if it's a drop-in replacement for a Duke, I want to know more!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tina||03-20-2001||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ramzy_steele:<BR><B>I dreading the horrible possiblility of being able to get whooped by my sister.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Sorry, but we women got to stick together.<BR>I'am voting for her.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tina.gif"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||lou_dias||03-21-2001||12:26 AM||||Hey, it's my experience that the v6 has a weak bottom end too.  Check out my thread in the mall regarding my 87 GT with a bad bottom end...||||reg||1
Z||000013||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-21-2001||02:22 AM||||The Iron Duke won't make more than about 150HP without some very expensive Super Duty parts.  If your sister has a 180HP duke, someone paid more for it than for the rest of the car.  The typical hopped-up duke motor with better cam and porting work done will make a lot less than 180 HP.<P>Can she lay one wheel burn outs through first, and well into second with a little chirp at third?  It seems like a 180HP Fiero would show signs of being a "one wheel wonder"?<P>Also, 180HP in a Fiero would give about 6.5 to 7.0 seconds 0 to 60.  This would feel like a Mustang 5.0L GT or an old IROC camaro.  Somehow, my gut says that her engine makes less than 180HP.<P>But, can you ask her what crate the motor came out of?  I don't think it was a GM crate motor as they only sold econo versions of the duke.  With one huge exception: the super duty engine.  A super duty crate motor sells for about $10-15K nowadays.<P>My guess is that a 5spd Formula that is healthy has no cause to fear an Iron Duke Fiero.<P>This post was made by a 1984 "hopped-up" duke automatic owner.  I may boast, but making my duke beat a V6 is something I have yet to achieve.  I have a Holley TBI and Manifold, MSD 6AL ignition with Accel coil, deburred head and exhaust manifolds, timing advanced to 10 degrees, aftermarket catalytic converter, and all new sensors, belts, hoses, alternator, starter, etc. <P>My guess is that I have about 120HP now.  With a torquey cam (I have an automatic), forged stroker crank, forged connecting rods, forged 9:1 compression pistons, and a fully ported head I may get my duke past 140HP but that will be after spending another $3K in parts.  The torque benefits might make it worthwhile for my automatic, but if it could beat a stick Formula it would be by a very small margin indeed.<P>I say buy the Formula, make sure it is running properly, then show your sister that her crate motor is not making 180HP.<P>If, on the other hand, she really has a Super Duty engine, then accept that any stock Fiero V6 stands no chance of beating it.  Also congratulate her on the extreme luck she has to own a super duty fiero.  They are unbelievable cars with the 16 vavle duke.  You're talking about 300HP in a 2500LB car.  That is a sick amount of power!||||reg||1
Z||000014||GoCartGuy||03-22-2001||01:18 AM||||If she's got the Super Duty, my condolences.  One thing I love about my tranny is the jump I get on a drag.  Got a  <BR>V-6 with a 3 speed auto tranny.  Don't drop out of first till 42 mph with my foot to the floor.  Chirp and boom, hook it up.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000015||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||03:55 AM||||I would have to join up with the skeptics about a 180 hp Iron Duke. Keeping the stock block and crank and doing a lot of tricks would bring it up to MAYBE around 140 hp but the crank would be under a lot of stress. <P>If she went with Super Duty then she easily spent more than the car's worth. I doubt though that the stock injectors "and everything else" could feed such output. Very few crate motors will put it out unless it was a specified Super Duty.<P>Call the bluff!!!!||||reg||6
Z||000016||Oreif||03-22-2001||10:36 AM||||I'm still betting it's not a duke but a Quad4. It's the only GM 4-cyl I could see getting 160-180 in a factory crate motor without getting into the very expensive SD4.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Black88GT||03-22-2001||11:33 AM||||Yeah those H.O. Quad 4's in the Beretta's could make 180 easy.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Kerri 98||05-25-2001||04:27 PM||||Well a few months have passed by, DID SHE BEAT YA? HAHAAHA...Sure the car makes some difference but the driver makes all the difference. I wanna know if all you saw was tail lights..Resent that comment Ricecooker, I would be willing to show you a winner...when u see my tail lights..haha||||reg||6
Z||000019||Songman||05-26-2001||03:36 AM||||I agree with Oreif and others that it must be a Quad4. Quad4s not only 'could' make 180, that is the stock hp on the HO model that I had in my Grand Am SE. I'm betting this engine is not a Duke at all....<P>Safe bet here for you... be a man, act like a man.. bluff your sister and never actually race her! Just tell her that she doesn't stand a chance... heheh<P>And I think we are lucky that Tina hasn't gotten Lisa and the rest of the ladies in here about all these comments on women driver's lack of skill!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon12.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||12
Z||000020||erikred||05-28-2001||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>The Iron Duke won't make more than about 150HP without some very expensive Super Duty parts.  If your sister has a 180HP duke, someone paid more for it than for the rest of the car.  The typical hopped-up duke motor with better cam and porting work done will make a lot less than 180 HP.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Where are you getting this from? 180 HP is fairly easy to obtain from a Duke.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>A super duty crate motor sells for about $10-15K nowadays.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well I guess if you are buying a 4 valve formula racing motor you may spend this much. But a 2 valve all aluminum engine with all the HP parts you could want only sells for about $6000 used.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>This post was made by a 1984 "hopped-up" duke automatic owner. I have a Holley TBI and Manifold, MSD 6AL ignition with Accel coil, deburred head and exhaust manifolds, timing advanced to 10 degrees, aftermarket catalytic converter, and all new sensors, belts, hoses, alternator, starter, etc. <P>My guess is that I have about 120HP now.  With a torquey cam (I have an automatic), forged stroker crank, forged connecting rods, forged 9:1 compression pistons, and a fully ported head I may get my duke past 140HP but that will be after spending another $3K in parts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>If you bought all of that just to make 120 HP you wasted your money. There is no reason the stock componets would not hold up to 120 HP. With some minor changes you could be making close to 200 HP. How the hell do you figure it would take another $3000 to get 20 HP? A $50 cam could do that. That is probably why you are only getting 120 HP now. You would have been better off saving the money you paid for the thottle body and intake for some headers and a good cam. This seems to be a mistake many people are making when rebuilding their Duke and trying to get a little extra oomph. They buy all the expensive bolt on parts that give small results (when used seperatly) and neglect the cheap HP that can be had by focusing on the block, head, and valve train. by shopping around anyone can build a reliable Duke with about 200 HP for about the cost of a good crate motor. That is assuming you do the work yourself of course. And you do not need a $1800 forged crank to do it.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000021||88formula||05-28-2001||12:38 PM||||To my knowledge no one has yet to build a reliable 200 horsepower from a production engine for his or her Iron Duke. A camshaft cost a lot more than $50 too. In fact the 92 horsepower Iron Duke I had in my coupe wasn’t even reliable. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula88||05-28-2001||01:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<BR><B> They buy all the expensive bolt on parts that give small results (when used seperatly) and neglect the cheap HP that can be had by focusing on the block, head, and valve train. by shopping around anyone can build a reliable Duke with about 200 HP for about the cost of a good crate motor. That is assuming you do the work yourself of course. And you do not need a $1800 forged crank to do it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>um, right.  And with just a bit of shopping around, attention to head work, etc., my 2.8 will make about 300hp.  The 3.4 pushrod's good for about 375.  Sure.  I'd buy that for a dollar.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000023||erikred||05-28-2001||06:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> um, right.  And with just a bit of shopping around, attention to head work, etc., my 2.8 will make about 300hp.  The 3.4 pushrod's good for about 375.  Sure.  I'd buy that for a dollar.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You should understand what I am saying before you making such stupid ass coments. Unlike some other members of this board, I research the subject before I jump to a conclusion. I never said you could double the engines power with some head work and a cam, or even the stock bottom end. <P>If the 2.5 block is so weak, prove it! Alot of people like to throw around the idea 'oh, it's weak and can only handle 140 HP'. What source is this stament based on? Show me what caused this to be fact. I can think of two different web sites where most people are getting most of there info. Do you believe everything just because it is on an internet site? A SBC is only rated for about 350 HP. Do you really believe a SBC cannot be built to handle 600 HP without going to a Bowtie block? Maybe I am worng about the 151 block, but show me some proof. Even if it is to weak I could still swap in a Chevy II or Mercuiser block (with some mods of course). I talked with Gerry at KRP and he tells me a mildly modified (10.5:1, cam, ect.) 3.0L should make about 225 HP. He says people use them for racing. Now who am I going to believe, someone who has experience with theses motors and even runs a company who makes SD blocks or a bunch of people who read a hotrod article off the web? I understand the Merc block is different, and I know it has more dispalcement, but I have run some combos through desktop 2000 and I know that "close to 200 HP" is possibly without going overboard. It would really suprise me if a block could not hold at least 75% more power than stock if not more. BTW, a 2.8 can make 300 HP.<P>And 88formula, you are correct no cam does cost $50, it is only $49.95. PAW sells two different ones. They are hydraulic flat but would still make a huge improvement.||||reg||13
Z||000024||p4n1c||05-28-2001||07:10 PM||||It's without a doubt in my mind an Olds Quad4 which is a 2.3L, to settle it, lift the decklid and look at the top of the engine.  <P>It will rape the stock 2.8L, and serve your ass on a platter.  With the stick it should put out 180hp/175lb-ft torque.  <P>It was the engine of choice for the Beretta GTZ among others and it was also the engine(slightly modifed for the '87 Olds Aerotech  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) that set the landspeed record for an internal combustion engine.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000025||88formula||05-28-2001||07:46 PM||||I have rebuilt one of those Iron Duke engines. Here is a connecting rod from a 91 Iron Duke that made 110 horsepower.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Img_0339.jpg">  See the bend in the rod! <BR>The connecting rods are weak and the block is a thin wall casting.<P>Be Careful. The Super Duty engine is not the same thing as the production engine. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
